In C programming, you can terminate a program in several ways depending on your needs. Here are some common methods:

1. **Using `exit()` Function:**
   The `exit()` function is part of the standard library and is used to terminate the program immediately. It takes an integer argument that can be used to indicate the status of the program.

   ```c
   #include <stdlib.h>

   int main() {
       // Your code here
       exit(0);  // Terminate the program with status 0 (success)
       return 0; // This line will not be executed if exit() is called
   }
   ```

2. **Using `return` Statement:**
   If you are inside a function (like `main` or any other function), you can use the `return` statement to exit the function and terminate the program.

   ```c
   int main() {
       // Your code here
       return 0;  // Terminate the program with status 0 (success)
   }
   ```

3. **Using `abort()` Function:**
   The `abort()` function is also part of the standard library and is used to terminate the program immediately without performing cleanup actions. It is generally used for debugging purposes.

   ```c
   #include <stdlib.h>

   int main() {
       // Your code here
       abort();  // Terminate the program immediately
       return 0; // This line will not be executed if abort() is called
   }
   ```

4. **Using Signal Handling:**
   You can handle signals using signal handling functions like `signal()` or `sigaction()`. For example, you can set up a handler for the `SIGINT` signal (which is typically generated by pressing Ctrl+C) to terminate the program.

   ```c
   #include <stdio.h>
   #include <signal.h>
   #include <unistd.h>

   void handle_sigint(int signum) {
       printf("Caught SIGINT\n");
       exit(0);
   }

   int main() {
       signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);  // Set up the signal handler

       while (1) {
           printf("Running...\n");
           sleep(1);  // Sleep for 1 second
       }

       return 0;
   }
   ```

